              "consequence": "A client supplying a prev cursor with negative offset causes a 500 crash in BasePaginator; while this requires a crafted cursor, the comment misleads maintainers into believing Django handles it.",
              "explanation": "The change to `BasePaginator.get_result` at lines 182–184 clamps `offset` to `max(0, offset)` only when `not cursor.is_prev`. When `cursor.is_prev` is True, `start_offset = offset` is used directly — the same as before the change. A prev cursor can carry a zero or negative `cursor.offset` (a prev cursor returned by the paginator has offset decremented by 1 each page, eventually reaching 0 or below). At offset 0 the slice `queryset[0:stop]` is fine, but a cursor constructed with a negative offset (e.g. by a client replaying a stale cursor) would again trigger Django's negative-indexing assertion on the unchanged path. The new comment 'This is safe because the underlying queryset will handle boundary conditions' is incorrect for this case as well.",
