              "anchor_line": 182,
              "category": "correctness",
              "consequence": "Upload size limits set by administrators are ignored by the client-side validation; users see wrong error messages and wrong pre-upload rejection behavior.",
              "explanation": "The change replaces `var maxSizeKB = Discourse.SiteSettings['max_' + type + '_size_kb']` with the hardcoded constant `10 * 1024`. This means the client-side size check no longer reflects the administrator's configured `max_image_size_kb` or `max_attachment_size_kb` settings. An admin who lowers the limit will still see users able to select and attempt to upload files up to 10 MB before getting a client-side rejection, and one who raises it above 10 MB will see spurious rejections. The same hardcoding appears in the 413-error handler (line 243).",
              "id": "finding-ea3ab1494aae0bc6d14f5dbd",
              "lines": [
                182,
                246
              ],
              "path": "app/assets/javascripts/discourse/lib/utilities.js",
              "severity": "p2",
              "title": "JavaScript file size check hardcodes 10 MB instead of reading the site setting"
            },
            {
              "anchor_line": 145,
              "category": "correctness",
              "consequence": "No current caller is shown passing two numeric values to `downsize`, so this does not break existing callers. However, the dead method is confusing and any future caller written to match its signature will silently use the wrong overload.",
              "explanation": "Ruby does not support method overloading. The file defines `def self.downsize(from, to, max_width, max_height, opts={})` at line 145 and then immediately redefines `def self.downsize(from, to, dimensions, opts={})` at line 149. The first definition is completely replaced by the second at class-load time and can never be called. The formatted-string logic in the first overload (`\"#{max_width}x#{max_height}\"`) is unreachable dead code; any future caller passing two numeric dimensions will have them passed as individual positional arguments to a method that interprets its third parameter as `dimensions` (a single string), producing wrong dimension formatting.",
              "id": "finding-922a0f379e5d8e5f7a7b6b23",
              "lines": [
                142,
                156
              ],
              "path": "app/models/optimized_image.rb",
              "severity": "p2",
              "title": "Dead first `downsize` definition is silently overridden by second in Ruby"
            }
          ],
